### /candidate/PROOF.md
     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied MPY semantics, `specialFilter` is partially correct for every
     6	arbitrary finite list of integers, with no bound on list length or integer
     7	magnitude. If the call terminates, it returns the number of input integers that
     8	are greater than 10 and whose first and last decimal digits are odd.
     9	
    10	The target claim loads the exact translated function body, resolves the
    11	`specialFilter`, `str`, and `ord` bindings through the real scope chain, creates
    12	and pops the real call frame, executes the real loop body, and constrains the
    13	final `<k>` result to `specialCount(INPUT)`. It also observes that the module
    14	binding remains, the local frame is removed, and the environment, heap,
    15	allocation counters, stack, return state, exception state, and exit code have
    16	their stated final values.
    17	
    18	This is a partial-correctness theorem, not a separate liveness theorem.
    19	
    20	## Formal claim
    21	
    22	The required target is `SPEC.special-filter` in `spec.k`:
    23	
    24	- Domain: `INPUT:ValSeq` satisfying `allInts(INPUT)`.
    25	- Program boundary: `#loadAll(Module(FuncDef(...)))` for the exact
    26	  `solution.mpy` body, followed by
    27	  `Call(Name("specialFilter"), list(INPUT))`.
    28	- Result: `specialCount(INPUT)`.
    29	- Observable final state: result, module binding, environment, scope counter,
    30	  heap, heap counter, stack, return state, exception state, and exit code.
    31	
    32	`specialCount` is structurally recursive over the entire symbolic `ValSeq`.
    33	Its disjoint step cases add one exactly when the projected integer is greater
    34	than 10 and both endpoint decimal character codes are odd. Decimal digit
    35	character codes have the same parity as their digit values because ASCII
    36	`'0'` has even code 48.
    37	
    38	`SPEC.filter-loop` is the loop circularity. In the exact reachable call
    39	context, it proves that running the loop over any remaining symbolic
    40	`INPUT:ValSeq` increases `count` by `specialCount(INPUT)`. Its empty and
    41	inductive cases collectively cover arbitrary finite inputs.
    42	
    43	## Proof-extension inventory
    44	
    45	No added rule bypasses a program-defined operation, call, loop, return, or
    46	state transition. No added trusted primitive is used.
    47	
    48	| Extension | Class and semantic role | Domain, context, state, and value justification | Dependents and validation |
    49	|---|---|---|---|
    50	| `filterBody()`, `specialFilterStmts()` | Definitional syntactic summaries (macros) | Expand to the exact constructors in `solution.mpy`; they name syntax and replace no execution. No cells are read or changed by macro expansion. | Both claims. Translator reproduction and the changed-body probe validate body sensitivity. |
    51	| `allInts` | Definitional summary | Total structural predicate: empty is true; a cons is integer-headed and recursively integer-only. Equations terminate and do not overlap. | Claim preconditions. The theorem quantifies directly over native `ValSeq`, not fixed sizes. |
    52	| `definedProjectInt`, `projectIntTotal`, the `#Ceil` characterization, cast orientations, collapse, and idempotence | Derived cast/projection lemmas | `projectIntTotal(V)` is usable only when the fixed sort predicate `isInt(V)` is entailed. The `#Ceil` equation connects it to K's partial `Val :> Int` cast; orientation rules preserve definedness. It reads no configuration cells and cannot manufacture an unguarded value. | The two dispatch twins and `specialCount`. Ground `[15]` and non-qualifying witnesses, plus the opposite-value probe, validate value sensitivity. |
    53	| Guarded `applyCmp(">", V, I)` twin | Derived lemma restating a frozen equation | Complete match is the pure function term under `isInt(V)`. Projection reduces to the same integer selected by the frozen `applyCmp(">", I1:Int, I2:Int) => I1 >Int I2` rule. It has no continuation or state footprint. | Symbolic `num > 10`. The original static rule and twin agree on overlap. |
    54	| Guarded `applyBuiltin("str", V, .Vals)` twin | Derived lemma restating a frozen equation | Complete match is the pure builtin function under `isInt(V)`. Its RHS is exactly the frozen `str(Int)` equation with the defined integer projection. It affects the digit branches but no state or control. | Symbolic `str(num)`. Ground LLVM execution and differential tests validate the concrete behavior. |
    55	| `firstDecimalCode`, `lastDecimalCode` | Definitional summaries | Exact endpoint selections from the fixed `Int2String`/`strToCodes` representation. Every integer string is nonempty, so index 0 and `length - 1` are valid. | Digit predicates and `specialCount`. Supported by K smoke execution and the independent decimal-string oracle. |
    56	| `firstDigitOdd`, `lastDigitOdd`, `isSpecial` | Definitional summaries | Exact modulo-2 predicates over endpoint decimal character codes. Total equations have no competing cases. | Human-facing property and recursive summary. |
    57	| `specialCount` equations | Definitional summary | Structural recursion decreases the `ValSeq`. Empty, non-integer, `<= 10`, first-even, last-even, and both-odd cases are exhaustive and pairwise disjoint. On the theorem domain only integer cases are reachable. | Both claims. The program-to-summary connection is proved by the loop circularity rather than assumed as a result lemma. |
    58	| `SPEC.filter-loop` | Derived auxiliary reachability claim / circularity | Matches the exact loop term, return continuation, active scope, module binding, call frame, empty heap, counters, return/exception state, and exit code reached by the target call. It executes the fixed loop body and changes only the locals shown in its RHS. | `SPEC.special-filter`. It is proved together with the target by the untrusted aggregate `kprove` command. |
    59	
    60	### Equation audit
    61	
    62	- All recursive definitions descend structurally.
    63	- Projection uses are guarded by `definedProjectInt`.
    64	- The dispatch twins agree with the frozen static equations on their overlap.
    65	- `specialCount` guards are exhaustive and disjoint: non-integer; integer not
    66	  greater than 10; greater than 10 with first digit even; first digit odd and
    67	  last digit even; or both odd.
    68	- No rule states or directly assumes the aggregate result of the program.
