   146	## Gate results
   147	
   148	### Gate A — PASS
   149	
   150	- A1: The exact function body and binding execute. Changing the initial count
   151	  from 0 to 1 invalidates the theorem and exposes actual result 1.
   152	- A2: There are no operational bridges. The circularity enumerates and
   153	  preserves the exact reachable configuration cells.
   154	- A3: Lookup, argument evaluation, call framing, builtin calls, loop control,
   155	  return, and frame pop execute under the fixed semantics. Dynamic Int dispatch
   156	  is connected to the fixed subsort rules by guarded total projection.
   157	- A4: The extension equations are terminating, covered, and consistent on
   158	  overlaps.
   159	- A5: The empty input is a realizable witness. Its false result mutation fails;
   160	  `[15]` also rejects the opposite value.
   161	
   162	### Gate B — PASS
   163	
   164	The HumanEval contract's material domain is arbitrary finite integer arrays:
   165	all negative, zero, positive, and arbitrarily large integers are included, and
   166	there is no list-length bound. Integers at most 10 and all negative integers are
   167	proved not to contribute. For integers greater than 10, `specialCount` states
   168	exactly the first/last odd decimal-digit property. The implementation, summary,
   169	and prompt examples agree.
   170	
   171	The task's “array of numbers” is interpreted as an integer array because the
   172	contract applies first and last decimal digits as integer properties. Python
   173	values of unrelated or non-integral classes are not silently included by the
   174	formal precondition.
   175	
   176	K `Int` and Python integers are both unbounded. The input is represented by the
   177	supplied semantics' documented read-only bare `list(ValSeq)` form; the program
   178	does not mutate it.
   179	
   180	### Gate C — PASS
   181	
   182	Every assumption and artifact is named below, all recorded commands are
   183	reproducible, and the empirical evidence uses an oracle independent of the
   184	proof equations.
   185	
   186	## Trust boundary
   187	
   188	| Trusted component | Influence | Dependents | Evidence |
   189	|---|---|---|---|
   190	| Supplied read-only MPY semantics | Defines Python execution, cells, list iteration, calls, operators, strings, and builtins | All claims | LLVM prompt-example smoke run and body/result mutations |
   191	| K compiler, Haskell backend, SMT reasoning, and reachability logic | Establishes `#Top` under the compiled theory | Formal proof | K v7.1.293; repeat aggregate runs |
   192	| K STRING hook `Int2String` and supplied `strToCodes` rules | Fix decimal integer character codes and therefore both digit predicates | `firstDecimalCode`, `lastDecimalCode`, `specialCount` | Ground K runs plus independent CPython decimal-string oracle |
   193	| Partial-correctness interpretation | Does not establish a separate termination theorem | Reported theorem scope | Explicitly stated here |
   194	
   195	There is no proof-local trusted primitive and no trusted proof claim.
   196	
   197	## Empirically supported facts
   198	
   199	`smoke.py` duplicates the exact solution body and checks both prompt examples
   200	and the empty input under LLVM. All assertions complete with `NoExc` and exit
   201	code 0.
   202	
   203	`test_solution.py` compares `solution.specialFilter` with an independent oracle
   204	that tests membership of the first and last decimal characters in
   205	`{"1","3","5","7","9"}`. Its 1006 cases comprise:
   206	
   207	- both prompt examples and the empty input;
   208	- every integer from -500 through 2000 in one list;
   209	- explicit threshold, endpoint-digit, and mixed cases;
   210	- integers with 80, 100, and 120 decimal places;
   211	- 1000 deterministic random lists of lengths 0 through 39 with values sampled
   212	  from `[-10^30, 10^30)`.
   213	
   214	It reports zero mismatches. This finite evidence supports the decimal
   215	representation bridge; the universal list theorem comes from `kprove`, not
   216	from testing.
   217	
   218	## Excluded behavior
   219	
   220	- Non-integer list elements are outside the integer-array contract and formal
   221	  precondition.
   222	- The theorem is relative to the supplied MPY semantics and its K hooks; it is
   223	  not a proof of the implementation of K or CPython.
   224	- No separate total-correctness or resource-bound theorem is claimed.
